  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**\n   *Central Idea*: Use simulated annealing (SA) combined with particle swarm optimization (PSO) to explore the solution space more effectively than local optimization alone. The SA will handle global exploration while PSO will provide focused, nature-inspired search. This hybrid method balances exploration and exploitation by allowing temporary acceptance of worse solutions at decreasing rates.\n   \n   *Key Improvements over previous*: \n   - Moves beyond sequential single-step optimization to parallel temperature management\n   - Better handles multimodal search space\n   - Uses hierarchical parameter adaptation based on resolution\n   - Includes adaptive cooling schedules sensitive to function landscape\n\n   *Bottleneck Addressed*: Current algorithm risks getting stuck in local optima. SA prevents this by occasionally accepting worse solutions, while PSO maintains population-based diversity. Progressive resolution doubling begins at feasible points found during early coarser optimization.\n\n2. **Mathematical Specification**\n   *Representation*: Continuous variables in [0,1]^n with piecewise constant projection\n   *Variables*: h \u2208 [0,1]^n such that \u222bh(x)dx\u22481\n   *Objective*: Minimize C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   *Constraints*: Integral normalization and domain boundaries\n\n   *Key Formulation*: Smoothed objective: \n   - f(h) = max_k (corr_k) + log(1+exp(\u03b2(\u03c6 - max_k corr_k)))\n   - where corr_k = (dx) * \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   - \u03c6 is a reference value from parent solution\n\n   *Derivative Handling*: Step-sizes adapt based on geometry using directional finite differences\n\n3. **Implementation Procedure**\n   *a.* **Initialization**: Start from parent constraints, interpolate for finer grids\n   *b.* **Temperature Schedule**: \n     - Base temperature: T = 5.0 * (n/140)**0.5\n     - Cool factor: \u03b1 = 0.995 per iteration until T_min=0.1\n   *c.* **PSO-Style Exploration**: 40 particles with velocity vectors\n     - Velocity roration: v_ij = \u03c9v_ij + c_r1u_ij + c_r2v_jk\n   *d.* **Repair Mechanism**: Project solution via:\n     - h_clip = np.clip(h,0,1)\n     - Sum adjustment: distribute deficit/malus proportional to local density\n   *e.* **Stopping Conditions**: \n     - Global stagnation: no improvement for 50 iterations\n     - Time expiration: track real runtime\n\n4. **Search Choices & Variations**\n   *Default Parameters*:\n   - \u03c9=0.7, c_r1=c_r2=1.4\n   - Initial population: semi-structured based on parent\n   - k-Neighborhood: consider nearest 10 solutions\n\n   *Adaptive Features*:\n   - Temperature adapts based on width-of-plateau\n   - Dimension jumps when PSO shows excessive clustering\n\n5. **Compute Budget & Robustness**\n   *Timeline*:\n   - Coarser grids (140 points): 400 seconds\n   - Medium grids (280 points): 400 seconds\n   - Finer grids (560 points): 300 seconds\n   *Safety Nets*:\n   - Reset velocities for stale particles\n   - Local simplex fallback for final solution\n   - Temperature floor at 0.1\n\n6. **Validation & Return Contract**\n   *Final Checks*:\n   1. Integral normalization: |sum(h) - n/2| \u2264 10^-4\n   2. Objective consistency: run validator_twice\n   3. Range validity: 0\u2264h_i\u22641\n\n   *Return Format*: \n   (h_projected, c5_score, n_optimal), where\n   - h_projected satisfies constraints\n   - c5_score = compute_c5(h_projected)\n   - n_optimal is grid resolution (560 if found)\n   \n   *Objective Tie-breaking*: Higher grid resolution first, then lower C5",
